WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.679
API keys need a lifecycle.

2
00:00:01.679 --> 00:00:10.331
Issuance should record owner, purpose, environment, scope, creation time, display behavior, approval path, and expected rotation path.

3
00:00:10.331 --> 00:00:17.627
A key should not appear in production without a record that explains why it exists and who is responsible for it.

4
00:00:17.627 --> 00:00:20.403
Keys should be shown only when appropriate.

5
00:00:20.403 --> 00:00:27.506
Many systems use display-once behavior for newly generated secrets so the full value is not retrievable later.

6
00:00:27.506 --> 00:00:32.994
That pattern reduces unnecessary exposure and encourages secure storage by the owner.

7
00:00:32.994 --> 00:00:39.063
If a system allows full retrieval later, the risk should be explicitly accepted and protected.

8
00:00:39.063 --> 00:00:41.517
Rotation should allow safe transition.

9
00:00:41.517 --> 00:00:48.813
In many systems, that means allowing overlapping old and new keys for a limited period while clients are updated.

10
00:00:48.813 --> 00:00:51.977
The overlap window should be visible and bounded.

11
00:00:51.977 --> 00:00:58.950
Operators should be able to tell which clients are still using the old key and when it is safe to remove it.

12
00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:02.824
Revocation should be fast enough for the risk of the system.

13
00:01:02.824 --> 00:01:06.698
Some keys can be disabled manually during normal operations.

14
00:01:06.698 --> 00:01:11.993
Others require near-real-time propagation across gateways, instances, and regions.

15
00:01:11.993 --> 00:01:18.321
The right design depends on what the key can access and how quickly the organization must respond.

16
00:01:18.321 --> 00:01:24.196
Expiration reduces stale credential risk but must be balanced with operational reliability.

17
00:01:24.196 --> 00:01:30.330
Short lifetimes can force better hygiene but may break integrations if owners are not prepared.

18
00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:36.852
Long lifetimes reduce maintenance pressure but increase the chance that forgotten keys remain active.

19
00:01:36.852 --> 00:01:47.441
Compromise response should include disabling the key, identifying usage, notifying owners, rotating dependent systems, reviewing logs, and documenting what changed.

20
00:01:47.441 --> 00:01:55.189
Stale keys, orphaned keys, test keys, and unused integration keys should be cleaned up through a regular review process.
